/// zenoh-bridge-ros2dds on a SIM host — a machine that runs one fm-sim run at a
/// time (local, fm-ws-01, or AWS), with the controller beside it.
///
/// The sim host is driven the way an Anvil workcell is: the desktop and `fm sim`
/// call the sim agent's `fm/sim/<host>/*` queryables, and the agent starts,
/// resets, and stops the run locally. So the bridge carries the run's state
/// outward and nothing else: no topic inward, no service, no action. The set
/// below is the sim topic list in README.md, and SIM_CONTRACT in
/// scripts/ci/check-transport.py grades this profile against that list in both
/// directions on every pull request.
///
/// Two hosts run it. A sim-only host (card workload `sim`) runs it as its one
/// bridge. A workstation that hosts sim runs keeps its own Humble bridge and
/// runs this profile as a SECOND bridge beside it, `fm-zenoh-bridge-sim.service`
/// with ROS_DISTRO=jazzy, because the sim image is Jazzy and a bridge only
/// discovers nodes of the distro it was told: Iron changed the GID layout in
/// `ros_discovery_info` from 24 bytes to 16, and the plugin routes a DDS writer
/// only once a node there has claimed it.
///
/// On a workstation both bridges share the namespace, so the desktop's
/// `<host>/**` subscription reads either, and differ in node name. Their keys
/// overlap — `/rosout` always, `/joint_states` and `/tf` too — and that is safe
/// because their sources do not: the Humble bridge misparses a Jazzy node's
/// discovery message (garbage past the 16-byte GID), and the Jazzy bridge reads
/// a Humble one as a node list of length zero (the plugin warns about exactly
/// this in its log), so each routes only its own distro's writers and no sample
/// crosses twice. Neither bridge subscribes to what the other publishes, so the
/// pair cannot loop either. What remains is the host's own business: a Humble
/// arm stack and a sim run publishing `/joint_states` at once would interleave
/// on one key, as they would on one DDS graph.
///
/// This file is a TEMPLATE. `scripts/install-bridge.sh` renders it and writes the
/// result to /etc/fm-comms/bridge.json5 on a sim host, and to
/// /etc/fm-comms/bridge-sim.json5 for a workstation's second bridge
/// (`./install.sh --role bridge-sim`). Committed files carry placeholders only —
/// never a real address. Read the render before installing it:
///
///     ./run.sh render bridge            on a sim host
///     ./run.sh render bridge-sim        on a workstation
///
/// Placeholders:
///   FM_ROUTER_ENDPOINT    the router, e.g. tcp/<rune>:7447. Fleet-wide, from
///                         /etc/fm-comms.env.
///   FM_RIG_NAMESPACE      this machine's ROS namespace. Derived from the machine
///                         identity card's name and typed nowhere: fm-ws-01
///                         becomes fm_ws_01, because a ROS name may not carry
///                         a hyphen.
///   FM_ROS_DOMAIN_ID      the DDS domain this machine's nodes run on. Fleet-wide,
///                         from /etc/fm-comms.env.
///   FM_BRIDGE_NODENAME    the bridge's DDS node name: zenoh_bridge_<namespace>
///                         as its host's one bridge, zenoh_bridge_<namespace>_sim
///                         as a workstation's second. Set by the render, from
///                         which of the two it is.
{
  mode: "client",
  connect: {
    endpoints: ["${FM_ROUTER_ENDPOINT}"],
  },
  scouting: {
    multicast: { enabled: false },
  },

  plugins: {
    ros2dds: {
      namespace: "/${FM_RIG_NAMESPACE}",
      nodename: "${FM_BRIDGE_NODENAME}",
      domain: ${FM_ROS_DOMAIN_ID},

      /// Allowlist, and ONLY an allowlist. zenoh-plugin-ros2dds flattens allow
      /// and deny into a single untagged choice, so a config carrying both is
      /// refused at startup as "unknown field `deny`" — which reads as a typo
      /// and is not one.
      ///
      /// The rule enforced by absence is the anvil's: there is no `subscribers`
      /// list at all, so no key on the fabric can reach a DDS writer here.
      /// `/commanded_ee_right` feeds the EE follower and the controllers take
      /// `joint_trajectory` directly, and both are commands only the run on this
      /// host may give. There is no `service_servers` list either: `/sim/reset`
      /// is reached only through the agent's `fm/sim/<host>/reset` verb, which
      /// calls it locally, so a reset always lands on the run the agent owns.
      ///
      /// `/sim/outcome` and `/tf_static` are TRANSIENT_LOCAL. The plugin keeps
      /// that without configuration: it discovers the writer's durability and
      /// serves late joiners from a per-route cache sized at the writer's
      /// history depth times `transient_local_cache_multiplier` (default 10).
      allow: {
        publishers: [
          /// Where the arm is, and the frames the viewer draws it in.
          "^/joint_states$",
          "^/tf$",
          "^/tf_static$",
          /// Sim time. Every node in a run takes `use_sim_time`, and the viewer
          /// stamps what it shows against the same clock.
          "^/clock$",
          "^/rosout$",
          /// Ground-truth object poses, the scene for the viewer, and one
          /// outcome per episode — by name, not a `/sim/.*` wildcard, so a new
          /// sim topic is a decision made here.
          "^/sim/objects$",
          "^/sim/markers$",
          "^/sim/outcome$",
          /// fm-skills' task events and perception detections, by name for the
          /// same reason: the topics fm-skills publishes today and no others.
          "^/task/events$",
          "^/perception/chest/can$",
          "^/perception/chest/paper$",
          "^/perception/wrist/can$",
          /// Chest and wrist cameras, compressed only. A raw frame is 20-50x
          /// the bytes and nothing on the fabric reads one.
          "^/cam_[a-z0-9_]+/image_raw/compressed$",
          "^/cam_[a-z0-9_]+/camera_info$",
        ],
      },

      /// A camera at its render rate is the largest thing this host could put
      /// on the wire, and the viewer draws well below it. Its intrinsics are
      /// capped with it: they never change, yet arrive once per frame.
      pub_max_frequencies: [
        "/cam_[a-z0-9_]+/image_raw/compressed=10",
        "/cam_[a-z0-9_]+/camera_info=10",
      ],

      reliable_routes_blocking: false,
    },
  },
}
